WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.514
Models should be managed as operational assets, not random files that accumulate over time.

00:00:04.514 --> 00:00:07.352
Names and tags help identify what is being used,

00:00:07.352 --> 00:00:09.559
but they are not a complete inventory.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:13.659
Teams still need documentation that explains which models are required, which are experimental,

00:00:13.659 --> 00:00:16.496
which are approved for shared use, which are deprecated,

00:00:16.496 --> 00:00:18.388
and which can be removed safely.

00:00:18.388 --> 00:00:21.226
Storage planning matters because model files can be large.

00:00:21.226 --> 00:00:23.749
A quick experiment may consume meaningful disk space,

00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:27.848
and several experiments can fill a workstation or shared server faster than expected.

00:00:27.848 --> 00:00:31.316
When storage runs out, model pulls can fail, applications can break,

00:00:31.316 --> 00:00:32.577
logs may stop writing,

00:00:32.577 --> 00:00:36.677
and operators may waste time figuring out which files are safe to delete.

00:00:36.677 --> 00:00:40.460
Pulling a model for a personal test is different from approving a

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.037
model for a team workflow.

00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:45.505
Shared use should include basic review: why the model is needed,

00:00:45.505 --> 00:00:49.605
what hardware it requires, what license or usage expectations apply, who owns it,

00:00:49.605 --> 00:00:52.127
and how it will be updated or retired.

00:00:52.127 --> 00:00:56.226
That review does not need to be heavy, but it should be visible.

00:00:56.226 --> 00:00:58.433
Good inventory discipline prevents confusion across machines.

00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:02.217
Operators should know where models are stored, how much space they consume,

00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:05.370
which hosts need which models, and what cleanup routine exists.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:10.100
Storage planning turns local AI from a collection of one-off downloads into a manageable environment.
